Lepton: x86_64 devices decode Opus in floating point (no flat-top clipping)

Android builds libopus fixed-point for every architecture, and Codec2's
c2.android.opus.decoder decodes with opus_multistream_decode to int16. In the
fixed-point build, decoded peaks above full scale (normal for loud, limited
masters after lossy coding) are saturated to flat tops at +/-32767, which is
heard as crackle on loud music. Apps that cannot load their own Opus decoder
on x86_64 (Metrolist ships libopusV2JNI.so for ARM only) use this decoder.

The float build's int16 decode soft-clips instead. Decoding the same 160 kb/s
packets of a -0.1 dBFS limited signal with this libopus: fixed-point gives
906 flat-topped runs (3542 full-scale samples per million), float gives none.

Build libopus as float for android_x86_64 only (SILK float sources instead of
fixed, without the fixed-only SSE4.1 files); host builds and other
architectures are unchanged. Android's integer overflow check in analysis.c
applies to fixed point only; float uses upstream's plain addition.

diff --git a/Android.bp b/Android.bp
--- a/Android.bp
+++ b/Android.bp
@@ -379,6 +379,75 @@
